WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.964
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:14.710
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.300
Welcome to "Eyes on
Enterprise," where

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.730
I bring on Googlers to
talk about the technology

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.190
landscapes that are
helping enterprises scale,

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.345
adapt, and modernize.

00:00:22.345 --> 00:00:24.220
My name is Stephanie
Wong, developer advocate

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.150
for Google Cloud.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.240
And today I have on Collin
Frierson, security specialist

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.120
for Google Cloud.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.203
And we're going to be
talking about cloud security

00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:33.757
and privacy, which is a
super highly-requested topic,

00:00:33.757 --> 00:00:35.090
so we have a lot to get through.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.480
But Collin is here to talk
about our comprehensive security

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.680
approach, all the
work that we've

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.180
been doing over
the last 20 years,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.290
and how you can leverage
some of those benefits.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.460
So Colin, thanks for being here.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.130
COLLIN FRIERSON:
Thanks for having me.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.550
STEPHANIE WONG: All right,
so diving straight into it,

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.830
I want to talk about
how a lot of companies

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:49.708
have concerns over
Cloud Security.

00:00:49.708 --> 00:00:50.750
They're still on premise.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.750
They're starting to migrate
some workloads that make sense.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.390
So, are there any
major differences

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.040
in how they should
approach security

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.780
on premise versus in the cloud?

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.710
COLLIN FRIERSON: The
biggest difference

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.150
is, when you're
in the cloud, you

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.952
get to share the responsibility
of security with the provider.

00:01:03.952 --> 00:01:05.410
So when you're on
premise, you have

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.410
to worry about everything
from provenance of hardware

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.200
all the way through what your
software is actually deployed.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.080
When you're using
a cloud provider,

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.290
you get to share
that responsibility.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.500
So there's a lot of
things you no longer have

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:15.917
to worry about,
depending on which

00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:17.050
services you're consuming.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.550
And that's a really good thing.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.430
Enterprises are far
more likely to be secure

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:24.250
if they have less things
to worry about and offer

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.792
those responsibilities
to a provider.

00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:28.375
STEPHANIE WONG: And of course,
a lot of the cloud technologies

00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:30.400
have matured over the
last five to 10 years.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.983
They still have
on-premise technology.

00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:35.560
So can you comment on how cloud
security has also matured along

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.930
with it or how we've
been able to adapt

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.422
to those new technologies?

00:01:39.422 --> 00:01:41.380
COLLIN FRIERSON: I think
the biggest difference

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.980
there is, because of the
size of investment cloud

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:48.190
has in both infrastructure
and supply chain,

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:50.830
cloud is able to solve a lot
of these problems fundamentally

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:52.570
differently than a
traditional enterprise would,

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.700
simply because of the
scale and nature of the way

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.020
you're buying.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.110
Google, for example, has been
building our own systems,

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:00.920
provenance from end to end.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.400
So we own the manufacturing
and the supply chain there.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.670
So we get to do things from
a supply chain logistics

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.590
perspective that other
companies don't do.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.900
They have to buy from a
traditional enterprise

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.282
vendor like Dell or HP.

00:02:13.282 --> 00:02:15.488
And you have to trust that
vendor then implicitly

00:02:15.488 --> 00:02:17.030
if you're buying
equipment from them.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:18.010
STEPHANIE WONG: So we've
talked about the technology

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:19.370
and landscape a little bit.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:20.807
But what about security teams?

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:23.140
Because obviously, people are
at the heart of a security

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.910
approach and so are the tools.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.770
Do you think it's more
important that you

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.960
have a comprehensive
security model that

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.800
includes both people and tools?

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.550
COLLIN FRIERSON: Tools are a
great way to augment people.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.050
But they're certainly
no substitute.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.350
So depending on the size
of the organization,

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.360
I'd probably have
very different advice.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.160
For the very large
organizations,

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.910
it's absolutely critical
that they invest in security.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.130
Most of my major
Fortune 500 companies,

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.475
it's a matter of
where they invest.

00:02:50.475 --> 00:02:52.600
So I try really hard to
make sure they're investing

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:53.590
in things that are effective.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.930
For smaller companies, it's
a matter of, will you invest?

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.347
And I'm trying
really hard to say,

00:02:57.347 --> 00:02:58.780
it is worth it to
make sure you're

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.438
investing in a security team.

00:03:00.438 --> 00:03:01.980
Even if it's just
a couple of people,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.045
I want them fully dedicated
and only doing security.

00:03:05.045 --> 00:03:06.670
Because they can make
a big difference,

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.680
and they can also make great
recommendations on what tools

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.160
they need, what risk the
company is likely to face.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.618
But you wouldn't
believe the number

00:03:12.618 --> 00:03:14.230
of mid and small
companies that have

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.600
half of a person
dedicated to security

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.600
and just hope
nothing goes wrong.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.600
We have a nice saying
internally here at Google.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.285
It's hope is not a strategy.

00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:24.160
STEPHANIE WONG: Definitely
not in securities.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.690
COLLIN FRIERSON: No, definitely
not in security, not even

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:26.680
for SREs.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.638
Hope is not a strategy.

00:03:27.638 --> 00:03:30.013
STEPHANIE WONG: One thing that
I've also heard and talked

00:03:30.013 --> 00:03:32.260
about with customer is
automating security practices

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.110
as much as possible-- like, for
example, building compliance

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:36.820
as a checkpoint in
your CIC process

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.770
before deploying,
things like that.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.320
That probably helps to create
a more lean security team.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:42.580
COLLIN FRIERSON: Absolutely.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.290
It is critical to have
automation in place there.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.220
A lot of times, people get
really worried when we say,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.980
oh, we're to automate
remediations to incidents.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.230
Because people go, well, wait.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.450
I don't want you turning things
off or shutting them down.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:56.867
I say, well, what
would an analyst

00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:00.148
do if they saw an incident
like this in their SIEM.

00:04:00.148 --> 00:04:01.690
Well, they'd go get
more information.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.130
OK, how do I
automatically get you

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.980
that information so you don't
have to manually go get it?

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.060
And if you know, well,
nine times out of 10,

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.200
I take this action, how can I
just take that action for you--

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.460
except in the one out of 10.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:15.220
Maybe I wake you up when
I take that exception

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.610
or when it doesn't make sense
that one time out of 10.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.350
So reducing the total
volume of events

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.558
that analysts have to respond
to is really important.

00:04:22.558 --> 00:04:24.760
And then trying to make
them as fast as possible

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:25.630
is also critical.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.090
It's almost as important
to filter out noise.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:31.442
So we know there's a lot of
security tools out there.

00:04:31.442 --> 00:04:33.400
And analysts can spend
just as much time trying

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.360
to filter through what is a
signal but not really an event.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.068
So automating that
process can be just as

00:04:39.068 --> 00:04:40.527
important to make
sure that they're

00:04:40.527 --> 00:04:42.940
spending their time on things
that are really critical.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.350
STEPHANIE WONG: It seems
like one underlying principle

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.790
that's important
here is creating

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.950
a more proactive security
practice as opposed

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.543
to reactive in those
cases, as well.

00:04:49.543 --> 00:04:50.710
COLLIN FRIERSON: Absolutely.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:52.578
Again, as you
shift to the cloud,

00:04:52.578 --> 00:04:54.370
your risk posture is
going to change, which

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:55.690
means your priorities change.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.420
And re-evaluating
that is something

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.150
that not everybody has
very much experience in,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.692
because enterprises are
within their first 10

00:05:03.692 --> 00:05:04.900
years of moving to the cloud.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.768
So re-prioritizing
what risks you're

00:05:06.768 --> 00:05:08.810
likely to face as a business
is really important.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.570
And then focusing your time
and energy on those things

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:11.420
is critical.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:11.980
STEPHANIE WONG: All
right, so phishing--

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.063
I know that's been a huge
topic in the last couple

00:05:14.063 --> 00:05:15.802
of for the start of breaches.

00:05:15.802 --> 00:05:18.010
How do we actually categorize
how severe of a problem

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.180
this actually is?

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.870
COLLIN FRIERSON:
It's a big problem.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.650
There's been several
high-profile breaches

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.320
in the last couple of years
that started with phishing.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.220
And we think about 85%
of all cyber crimes

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.147
initiate via phishing.

00:05:31.147 --> 00:05:32.980
STEPHANIE WONG: So I've
heard of these tests

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.930
that I know I've seen
in my past companies

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.490
as well, where they
send you an email

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.830
to try to test your
employees and see if you'll

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:40.660
give any log-in information.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.010
Do people still use that?

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:42.600
Does it work?

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.100
COLLIN FRIERSON:
You'd be surprised.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.620
Everybody still does those.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.050
But we have not
seen great results

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:50.920
in terms of getting people
to be better at this.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.810
It turns out,
about 18% of people

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.760
will fail those
exams every time.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.105
It's just a question of
which percentage failed.

00:05:57.105 --> 00:05:59.480
Well, while companies still
certainly do those exercises,

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.290
I'm not a big fan.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:02.840
I don't think they actually
make the company any safer.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:03.430
STEPHANIE WONG: Can
you talk about what

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.090
we do here at Google?

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.190
Because I know we have a
different approach to that.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:07.107
COLLIN FRIERSON: Yeah.

00:06:07.107 --> 00:06:10.510
So our approach has been the
employee cannot be relied upon

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:12.730
to not put their credential
into a phishing site.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.100
So we had to build
technology to solve

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.750
the problem for the
employees to keep them safe.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.730
So we built a technology that's
called a hardware security key.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:25.000
It's based on an open
source standard called FIDO.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.440
And we require that everyone
use these here at Google.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:29.732
So whether you're an employee,
contractor, or a vendor,

00:06:29.732 --> 00:06:30.770
you have to have one.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.610
And it has driven our
incident rate to zero.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:36.940
STEPHANIE WONG: So, what's a
bad example of someone suffering

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:38.080
from a phishing attack?

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:40.780
Have they actually lost money
from this kind of problem?

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.197
COLLIN FRIERSON:
Yeah, actually, I

00:06:42.197 --> 00:06:45.070
worked with a hospital
whose CFO was phished.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.920
And the phisher,
what they did was

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.600
they emailed their purchasing
department and said,

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.270
hey, I need a purchase order.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.550
We are trying to
close a M&amp;A deal.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:56.860
Wire $80,000 to this
account really quick.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:58.450
And I'd be so grateful.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.340
Please email me if you
have any questions.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.900
And the accounting department,
trying to be helpful, agreed.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.400
So they did what the CFO asked,
even though it wasn't really

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:06.900
the CFO.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.980
It was the phisher--

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:11.530
and all because he lost
access to his email.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.560
And so $80,000 gone
just like that--

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.503
and it happened twice--
not once but twice.

00:07:16.503 --> 00:07:17.920
STEPHANIE WONG:
At the same place?

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:18.610
COLLIN FRIERSON:
At the same place.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:18.910
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:19.990
COLLIN FRIERSON: And
after the second time,

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.265
they give us a call and said,
we really need help fixing this.

00:07:22.265 --> 00:07:23.440
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, this might
be a problem at this point.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.950
COLLIN FRIERSON: This is
getting really expensive.

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.020
STEPHANIE WONG: So tying
this back to cloud,

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.627
I know that you can obviously
use this with Google Cloud.

00:07:28.627 --> 00:07:31.210
But can a company implement this
into their production running

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:31.933
systems, as well?

00:07:31.933 --> 00:07:33.100
COLLIN FRIERSON: Absolutely.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:34.600
So it's an open standard.

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:38.670
So Google, of course, supports
it in every account we offer.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.170
Other major cloud providers are
picking up support, as well,

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:42.640
in various forms.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:45.560
And keys are designed
to be used many to many.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.533
So if you have one key,
you can use it anywhere

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:48.700
on the web that supports it.

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:51.475
And the number of places
supporting is going up rapidly.

00:07:51.475 --> 00:07:54.100
STEPHANIE WONG: We talked about
the shared responsibility model

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.170
when it comes to security.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:57.670
I know a lot of
people have concerns

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:58.712
over moving to the cloud.

00:07:58.712 --> 00:08:00.850
And you mentioned that
it can actually help

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:02.260
a company's security posture.

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:04.510
Can you talk more about
how it can actually help?

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.427
COLLIN FRIERSON: Typically
things, enterprises

00:08:06.427 --> 00:08:09.310
have an entire staff of
teams just to do patching.

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.013
And as you move on
to cloud services,

00:08:11.013 --> 00:08:12.430
many of the managed
services don't

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:13.520
require you to do that at all.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.228
It's the cloud provider's
responsibility.

00:08:15.228 --> 00:08:16.990
Great example for
Google is actually

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:19.960
the Heartbleed
vulnerability back in 2014--

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:23.330
affected everyone on the
internet, was really scary.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:25.330
Google actually had a
team that discovered this.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.610
So we found it.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:32.169
We helped the Red Hat develop
a fix back at the time.

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:35.919
And we actually had the entire
system patched in 46 hours--

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:37.750
so all of Google's
infrastructure,

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.400
multiple places.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.440
And that was a huge
deal, especially because,

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.862
when you look at the rest of
the internet, 90 days later,

00:08:44.862 --> 00:08:46.570
still about 30% of
the internet was still

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:47.800
susceptible to that risk.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:48.460
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, it ties back

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:50.173
to what were saying
earlier about having

00:08:50.173 --> 00:08:52.090
the resources, and the
network, and everything

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.465
that we have here to be able
to run through these changes

00:08:54.465 --> 00:08:55.450
as quickly as possible.

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:55.640
COLLIN FRIERSON: Yeah.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.640
Well, and Google,
again, we invest.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.440
So we understand
that our business

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.440
is providing world-class
secure infrastructure.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.190
So when there's a
vulnerability like that,

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:05.470
we have all hands on deck, and
we've trained, and gotten ready

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:06.050
for this.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.133
STEPHANIE WONG: So companies
have a lot of concern

00:09:08.133 --> 00:09:10.438
over cloud providers
potentially using their data

00:09:10.438 --> 00:09:11.480
or their customer's data.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.865
So what can we do to guarantee
that that doesn't happen?

00:09:13.865 --> 00:09:15.490
COLLIN FRIERSON: It's
a common concern.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:18.580
I would break it down into
three classes of things

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.130
we do to try to help.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:21.130
The first one is audits.

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.380
So we go through
really rigorous audits

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.050
to demonstrate that
we're not using data

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:29.530
for any purposes other
than to render the service.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:33.070
So specifically, it's
like ISO 27018, which

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.600
is a cloud privacy standard.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.840
The second one is
every cloud provider

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:40.460
has contractual
clauses that say,

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:42.460
we will only use the data
to render the services

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:43.658
as described.

00:09:43.658 --> 00:09:45.200
And the data belongs
to the customer.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.935
So Google has no intellectual
property rights contractually.

00:09:47.935 --> 00:09:49.810
One of the other things
we launched last year

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:52.060
was a service called
Access Transparency,

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.520
which is a technical control.

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.490
And what that allows
us to do is produce

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.460
logs any time a Google
administrator needs

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.190
to enter a customer's
tenant to perform something,

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:05.757
like whether it's a port
request or maintenance.

00:10:05.757 --> 00:10:07.840
But we show technical
evidence of that every time,

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.420
and it's immutable.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:13.010
We were first in industry
to do something like that.

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:14.735
And also available
is now a customer

00:10:14.735 --> 00:10:17.110
can control what types of
access they're going to permit.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:18.610
Maintenance is OK,
but support's not

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:20.960
OK for a given set
of their environment.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:21.820
STEPHANIE WONG: So when we
were talking about phishing,

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:23.502
you mentioned the security keys.

00:10:23.502 --> 00:10:25.960
And that's part of a larger
picture of our overall security

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.523
philosophy, something
called defense in depth.

00:10:28.523 --> 00:10:30.190
COLLIN FRIERSON: So
the defense in depth

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:34.690
is how we think about
defending ourselves from really

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:36.520
sophisticated adversaries.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:38.950
So what we do is we
think about a problem.

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:39.820
We build a control.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.680
And we build it as
the best we can.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.650
And then the next
step is once we

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:46.760
think it's perfect to
assume it's broken.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:47.890
So let's assume this
is going to break

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:49.690
in a way we can't understand
or figure out yet,

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:51.700
and we have to build
another separate, entirely

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:55.310
independent control accounting
for this one is now broken.

00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:57.880
So when we're thinking
about a problem set,

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:00.370
we want there to be
multiple things that

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:02.560
have to all fail simultaneously
in order for data

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:03.198
to be exposed.

00:11:03.198 --> 00:11:04.990
So that's what we mean
by defense in depth.

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:07.242
STEPHANIE WONG: And that
also means, especially even

00:11:07.242 --> 00:11:08.950
internally at Google,
we don't use a VPN,

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:10.840
because we have all
these layers in place

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.850
to prevent any bad
actor from being

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.320
able to access
beyond what they're

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:15.730
supposed to be able to access.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:16.120
COLLIN FRIERSON: Right.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:18.078
VPNs would be, like, a
single point of failure.

00:11:18.078 --> 00:11:20.435
It's much better to have
10 browser tabs, which

00:11:20.435 --> 00:11:22.060
means 10 different
encryption channels,

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:24.137
in order to send data
independently to each one.

00:11:24.137 --> 00:11:25.360
STEPHANIE WONG: One of
the interesting things

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:27.370
that developers care
about often is being

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:28.837
able to have cloud governance.

00:11:28.837 --> 00:11:30.670
So they want to be able
to work productively

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:32.587
in the cloud, leverage
things like Kubernetes,

00:11:32.587 --> 00:11:34.150
and cloud native technologies.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:37.060
Yet, security teams also care
about maintaining governance

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:40.120
and creating card
guardrails in place.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.640
So, how can you have a
balance between both?

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:45.470
COLLIN FRIERSON: Yeah, this
is the practice of DevSecOps.

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:48.370
The basic premise is you want
to make sure that your security

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:50.150
team is very close
to your developers,

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:52.540
so they really understand
what tools they're using,

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:54.790
what they need to be productive,
and the security team

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.017
is not blocking them by
writing a policy that

00:11:57.017 --> 00:11:59.350
doesn't get looked at until
someone screams loud enough.

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:01.690
Having security team members
close to those developers

00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:03.400
is key, because
they'll understand

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.170
what they're blockers
are, what they

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:07.378
need to do to move forward,
and where they can strike

00:12:07.378 --> 00:12:10.205
a balance between what's risky
to the company versus what's

00:12:10.205 --> 00:12:12.580
actually going to make the
company productive, and money,

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:13.640
and all that fun stuff.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:15.765
STEPHANIE WONG: I think,
with a lot of enterprises,

00:12:15.765 --> 00:12:18.130
they have a lot of
organizational structures

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:19.300
in place already.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.830
So how have you
seen companies be

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:23.380
able to restructure that
so that they can work

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:24.588
more closely with developers?

00:12:24.588 --> 00:12:26.297
COLLIN FRIERSON: The
best teams I've seen

00:12:26.297 --> 00:12:28.720
are the ones where the security
person is embedded and has

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:31.090
a dotted line report to
the developer's manager.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:32.373
So they have the same goals.

00:12:32.373 --> 00:12:33.790
They go to the
same team meetings.

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:36.340
But they also get security
saying, hey, don't let

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:38.230
them go off the reservation.

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:42.280
We still need to make sure that
the guardrails are in place

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.478
and we're following this
basic operational model.

00:12:44.478 --> 00:12:46.270
STEPHANIE WONG: So
companies obviously want

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:48.910
to maximize protecting their
sensitive data on the cloud.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:50.650
And they take
advantage or have heard

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:53.170
of things like Zero
Trust, parameter lists.

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:55.905
Do we employ those kind of
concepts here at Google Cloud?

00:12:55.905 --> 00:12:57.280
COLLIN FRIERSON:
So last year, we

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.460
launched a service that lets
you build perimeters round

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:04.270
your APIs, which, again,
is much easier to do

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:07.370
than trying to manage
perimeters around IP addresses.

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:09.855
And we also have,
again, layering controls

00:13:09.855 --> 00:13:11.230
on that defense
in depth strategy

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:14.260
to make sure that, in order
to access sensitive data,

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:16.302
there must be five gates
that are passed through.

00:13:16.302 --> 00:13:18.135
But we can still check
for all of the things

00:13:18.135 --> 00:13:20.020
that an enterprise
security team would like.

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:20.980
What device are you on?

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:22.270
Where are you coming from?

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:23.585
What time of day is it?

00:13:23.585 --> 00:13:25.210
Have I seen you on
this device before--

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:26.110
things like that.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:27.280
STEPHANIE WONG: I want to
talk a little bit about some

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:30.430
of the announcements we heard
at Next last year and things

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:32.440
like Access Transparency,
which you mentioned,

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:33.907
and external key management.

00:13:33.907 --> 00:13:34.990
Do you mind going into it?

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:35.907
COLLIN FRIERSON: Sure.

00:13:35.907 --> 00:13:38.290
So this is all about us
giving evidence to customers

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:41.230
that their data is not being
used by Google for any purposes

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:42.700
other than to render services.

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:45.610
External Key Manager is really
targeted for major enterprises.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:48.910
So it's a way for them to
retain ownership of their keys

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:50.410
on premise while
still interacting

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.180
with Cloud Services.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:54.310
It's a first in
industry, where they

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:57.980
have to maintain their existing
key infrastructure on prem.

00:13:57.980 --> 00:13:59.980
But we actually get to
programmatically interact

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:02.770
with that system through cloud,
which means, if at any point

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.080
they decide they don't
want to use cloud anymore

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:08.350
and they turn off their
key services on prem,

00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:10.570
all the cloud services
immediately stop working.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:11.530
STEPHANIE WONG: We can't
talk about security

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:12.940
without talking
about compliance.

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:14.320
We know that
different industries

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:17.510
like banking versus health care
has different regulatory needs.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:19.720
So how are we able to
meet those concerns?

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:21.220
COLLIN FRIERSON:
Yeah, so at Google,

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:24.850
we decided everyone
should have access

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:26.660
to secure infrastructure.

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:28.870
So we built Google
Cloud on top of what

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:31.330
we consider the most technically
sophisticated and secure

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:33.700
infrastructure and then
built a compliance and audit

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.210
program on top of that.

00:14:35.210 --> 00:14:39.280
So it means, whether you're in
health care, or life sciences,

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.450
or in banking, or
even in government

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:43.960
in the federal space, we can
meet your regulatory needs

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:45.208
with the same platform.

00:14:45.208 --> 00:14:46.750
STEPHANIE WONG:
Regulatory compliance

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:49.738
is obviously one way that we
instill trust in our customers.

00:14:49.738 --> 00:14:51.280
But we also have to
meet the changing

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:52.900
legal needs of each country.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:54.700
I'm sure those
constantly change.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.810
So, how are we putting an
effort into adapt to those?

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:00.460
COLLIN FRIERSON: Yeah,
this is a huge effort.

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:03.190
Whether it's regulation
and rules in the US,

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:05.950
versus Australia, the
EU, Japan, every country

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:09.280
has rules about use
of data and privacy.

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.910
And Google's stance
here is offering

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:15.080
the most secure and
private platform

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.620
we can and, again, meeting
the regulations once we've

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:19.460
done that.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:21.710
So in most cases,
like in the GDPR,

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:24.055
for example, most of the work
we had already been doing.

00:15:24.055 --> 00:15:25.430
And it was a matter
of, well, how

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:29.030
do we acertain show this to
the EU regulatory bodies?

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:30.240
Same thing for HIPPA--

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.468
we already take data privacy
more seriously than HIPPA does.

00:15:33.468 --> 00:15:35.510
We just need to demonstrate
that what we're doing

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:37.790
meets the spirit and the
technical requirements

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:38.733
of those rules.

00:15:38.733 --> 00:15:41.150
STEPHANIE WONG: Now, coming
from a regulatory perspective,

00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:43.220
how do companies actually
adapt to those changing

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.307
legal requirements,
like GDPR, CCPA, those?

00:15:46.307 --> 00:15:48.140
COLLIN FRIERSON: The
cloud provider actually

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:49.790
helps quite a bit,
because they take

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:53.167
on some of the regulatory risk
and can help with compliance.

00:15:53.167 --> 00:15:55.750
Although, we certainly don't do
everything for the enterprise.

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:58.042
The enterprises I've seen
that have been really worried

00:15:58.042 --> 00:16:00.380
about this are very quickly
accelerating their adoption

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:02.630
to cloud, because they can
now share this liability

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:03.720
with the cloud provider.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:05.150
STEPHANIE WONG: All right,
so we just talked about a lot

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:07.520
here with contracting and
regulatory requirements.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:09.860
But bringing it back
to the larger picture,

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.110
a lot of the sophistication
of our adversaries

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:13.400
is constantly increasing.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:14.900
And we have a lot
of tools out there

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.127
that you can leverage to
protect your environment.

00:16:17.127 --> 00:16:18.710
What can you say to
an enterprise that

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:21.170
just simply wants to keep
up with that dangerous world

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:22.033
out there?

00:16:22.033 --> 00:16:23.450
COLLIN FRIERSON:
Yeah, the big one

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:26.420
is, get the cloud provider
to help you with as much

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:28.610
as you can take on.

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:33.050
Most enterprises' core business
is not security and technology.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:36.980
So if that sounds like you, make
the cloud provider do as much

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:38.790
of it for you as you can.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:40.610
And then try to reduce
the number of tasks

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.718
that you as an enterprise
are responsible for.

00:16:42.718 --> 00:16:44.510
So make the cloud
provider encrypt for you.

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.910
Make the cloud provider
provide systems

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:48.922
that our GDPR
compliant, so you can

00:16:48.922 --> 00:16:51.380
write policies that are easier
to enforce, rather than have

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:52.380
to enforce systems, too.

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.338
STEPHANIE WONG: So, what
can a company actually

00:16:54.338 --> 00:16:57.000
do today so that they can
start improving their security

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:57.500
posture?

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:59.600
Or those who are already
bringing on Google Cloud, what

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:01.610
can they do to start
leveraging some of the tools

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:02.540
that you've mentioned?

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:04.107
COLLIN FRIERSON:
For cloud customers,

00:17:04.107 --> 00:17:06.440
the thing I would recommend
is log into Security Command

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:07.369
Center.

00:17:07.369 --> 00:17:10.900
And start going through what
tools we have available there.

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:12.470
It's a great way
to look at, gosh,

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:14.430
did I misconfigure something?

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:18.020
Do I have environments that
have a known exploit that's

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:19.730
currently active?

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:22.250
And we provide a lot
of those signals out

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.520
of the box in the platform.

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:24.690
So start there.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:26.359
It's a great way
to start triaging.

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:29.130
And also, take a look
at our security blog.

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:30.530
So we publish periodically.

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:33.710
What are some of the best
practice for enterprises there

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:35.210
and how to mitigate
certain threats.

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:35.870
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
thank you so much.

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:36.607
We covered a ton.

00:17:36.607 --> 00:17:38.190
So I appreciate you
being on the show.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:39.480
COLLIN FRIERSON: Thank
you for having me.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.647
STEPHANIE WONG: So everyone,
if you have any stories

00:17:41.647 --> 00:17:43.700
or anecdotes about your
own security challenges,

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:45.360
how you've been able
to address them,

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:46.430
or if you've been
able to use some

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:48.030
of the tools we
talked about today,

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:49.320
let us know in the comments.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:49.910
Take care.

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:51.868
And join us next time
for "Eyes on Enterprise."

00:17:51.868 --> 00:17:54.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

